{
  "description": "issue #738 (maintainer ruling): the cheapest proof that `clear_screen`'s homing no longer depends on the ORDER the turtles were listed in. spec/turtles-and-sprites.md:113 makes it explicit — `clear_screen`'s homing \"is ordinary per-turtle movement and applies once for each addressed turtle, so the result never depends on the order the turtles were listed in: `tell [ :a :b ]` and `tell [ :b :a ]` home the same two turtles\". The program runs the identical move/turn/clear_screen sequence twice, once under each ordering. Before the ruling the two halves disagreed: \"current\" was the FIRST member of the addressed set, so the first half homed :a and left :b at [0 10] heading 45, and the second half did the reverse. Here both halves home BOTH turtles — each emits one homing pair per addressed turtle (`move` [0 10] -> [0 0] reporting the pre-reset heading 45, then `turn` 45 -> 0, stamped with that turtle's `turtle_id`) followed by exactly ONE un-stamped `clear`, and each half's trailing `each` prints [0 0] and 0 for both turtles. The only difference between the halves is the order the per-turtle events appear in, which follows the addressed set's order for every per-turtle command (`forward`/`right` above show the same reordering) — the RESULT is identical. Every change of the addressed set — the two `tell`s, each `each` iteration, and each restoration — also emits an addressing `primitive` event carrying `addressing: { addressed_turtle_ids, current_turtle_id }` (issue #766, spec/rendering.md:193).",
